  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 220 PM Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Anomalously hot temperatures expected to \npersist through Thursday and perhaps into Friday.\n\nA deep subtropical ridge of around 595 dam centered over the \nMid-Mississippi Valley in the 12z H5 RAOBs this morning is \nforecast to strengthen as it wobbles gradually eastward and \nstretching over the Carolinas by Thurs morning. Highly anomalous\nH850 temperatures (at-or-above the 99.5 percentile) initially \ndirected over the Mid-Mississippi Valley today, will gradually \nwarm to around 20-22C and slowly be directed over the southern \nMid-Atlantic Wed through Fri. This pattern is highly favorable \nfor an early September heat wave with minimal convective chances\ndue to influence from the strong subtropical ridge. \n\nLow-lvl thicknesses and diabatic techniques line up with \nstatistical guidance and supports widespread mid/upper 90s as \nearly as Tues, but especially Wed into Fri. A few locations may \nreach triple digit air temperatures, but widespread GTE 100 \ndegrees is not expected. Heat index values in the upper 90s to \naround 105 degrees are expected each day with areas south and \neast of Raleigh most probable to surpass 105 degrees for several\nhours due to better low-lvl moisture in place. Targeted heat \nadvisories appear likely. Increasing convective chances, \nan approaching cold front and northeast flow in its wake should\nput an end to the heat by Sun.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Labor Day weekend through Monday gradually \ntrends cooler and drier with a lingering chance of showers and \nstorms.\n\nEnsemble guidance is indicating that the hot anomalous ridge of\nhigh pressure over the area will gradually shift westward this \ncoming holiday weekend. Much of the guidance is projecting the \nridge will shift into the Midwest to Central Plains of the US by\nSaturday. \n\nAs the ridge shifts west, troughing across the Mid-Atlantic and\nNE US is favored to take over. This troughing will be \nassociated with a cold front that approaches Saturday and is \nforecast to push through by Sunday. Depending on how the pattern\nevolves, some models suggest cool 1026-mb high pressure could \nextend into the area by Labor Day, favoring highs for the latter\npart of this weekend and into Monday in the low to middle 80s. \nDewpoints could dip into the 50s on Monday, a welcome change.\n\nAhead of the front, we should see storm chances increase. Those\nchances may initially start Friday with a pre-frontal trough, \nbut better coverage is expected Saturday ahead of the front. \nIsolated or scattered storms could linger into Sunday, though \ndrier conditions are favored as we head into Labor Day. Right \nnow, we are not outlooked from SPC. However, their long-range \ndiscussion does indicate a possible severe risk over the \nCarolinas Saturday ahead of the front. Predictability, however, \nis too low at this time, but will be monitored as we go through \nthe week.",
